  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 2,
  "estimatedSeconds": 180,
  "stem": "Nyasha has four pairs of values of $x$ and $y$ and suspects $y = kx^{n}$. Explain how she can use her data to check this, and how she then finds $n$ and $k$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "Plot ln y against ln x (or compute them): the law holds if the points lie (close to) a straight line",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "n is the gradient of the line and ln k its intercept on the ln y axis, so k = e^(intercept)",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "markingNotes": "Example answer: She calculates ln x and ln y for each pair and plots ln y against ln x. If the points lie on (or close to) a straight line, the law fits, since ln y = n ln x + ln k. The gradient of the line of best fit is n, and its intercept is ln k, so k = e raised to the intercept."
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "A straight line is the test; gradient and intercept give the constants.",
  "hints": [
   "What does taking ln of y = kxⁿ give?"
  ],
  "skill": "EVALUATION",
  "calculator": false
 },
